<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/5th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Thermopylae</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/480-bce">480 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Also known as Thermopylae · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-persian-wars">Greco-Persian Wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Thermopylae was fought in 480 BCE at a narrow coastal pass in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, where a small Greek force led by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonidas-i">Leonidas of Sparta</a> held off the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a> for several days. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> eventually outflanked the defenders and destroyed them. The battle allowed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> to advance into central Greece, but it became an enduring symbol of resistance.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae.jpg" alt="Battle of Thermopylae" width="320" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jacques-Louis David · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L%C3%A9onidas_aux_Thermopyles_-_Jacques-Louis_David_-_Mus%C3%A9e_du_Louvre_Peintures_INV_3690_;_L_3711.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its campaigns included Cyrus's conquest of Lydia, where camels were reportedly deployed to unsettle the Lydian cavalry, the conquest of Egypt (525 BCE), Darius's Scythian campaign (c. 513 BCE), and Xerxes's massive invasion of Greece (480 BCE), which brought victory at <b>Thermopylae</b> and the sack of Athens.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Each people sent two delegates, the hieromnemones, to meetings held each spring and autumn at Delphi and at Anthela near <b>Thermopylae</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delphi">Delphi · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Thucydides reports that his host was said to number about 150,000 men, a third of them cavalry; the figure is probably inflated, but the army alarmed the Greeks as far south as <b>Thermopylae</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/odrysian-kingdom">Odrysian Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 480 BCE Leonidas I held the pass of <b>Thermopylae</b> with a small Greek force including 300 Spartiates until he was outflanked and killed, and in 479 BCE the regent Pausanias commanded the Greek army that destroyed the Persian land forces at the Battle of Plataea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sparta">Sparta · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The singer recalls learning as a boy of the ancient fighters for freedom, the Spartans at <b>Thermopylae</b> and the Horatii, and dreams of the day when Ireland will be free.</blockquote><a href="/songs/a-nation-once-again">A Nation Once Again</a></li><li><blockquote>This one began at the shrine of Demeter at Anthela near <b>Thermopylae</b> and extended its care to Delphi.</blockquote><a href="/state/amphictyonic-league">Amphictyonic League</a></li></ul></section>
